    <p class="blog-article-lede">This replaces an earlier launch story that made unsupported novelty and competitor claims. The useful fact is simpler: sixteen tool pages were added across AI security, detection engineering, web security, and developer utilities.</p>

    <div class="blog-body">
      <h2>What changed</h2>
      <p>The set included pattern-based prompt and agent-configuration reviews, an LLM-request privacy scan, model-metadata checks, a static AI cost table, SIEM-query translation helpers, security configuration builders, and other focused utilities. Each page describes its inputs, output, and known limitations.</p>

      <h2>How to read the labels</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Browser:</strong> the operation runs in the page.</li>
        <li><strong>Edge:</strong> submitted input goes to a Cloudflare Worker operated by Penumbra Forge.</li>
        <li><strong>Network:</strong> the browser contacts the named destination or third-party API.</li>
        <li><strong>AI · routing varies:</strong> WebGPU stays in-page after model download; configured-endpoint modes send prompts and relevant content to that URL.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Limits</h2>
      <p>Pattern scanners and generated translations are review aids, not verdicts. Pricing tables become stale. AI output depends on the selected model. A tool being available without signup says nothing about whether equivalent tools exist elsewhere, and this release note makes no such claim.</p>

      <p>Browse the current catalog at <a href="/tools/">Tools</a>; each live page carries the current disclosure rather than relying on this dated announcement.</p>
    </div>
  </article>
